+{"q": 11, "question": "Who is the most likely murderer?", "narrative": "In the eerie silence of a roller rink, a man named Clarence met his startling end by a trident, pulling Detective Winston into a web of enmity and suspicion involving two unusual suspects, Unity and Vivian.\n\nThe flashing lights of the roller rink danced in a kaleidoscope of colors across Winston's stern face. He always had a knack for ending up in the most bizarre crime scenes, and this one was no different: A man named Clarence ended up dead at a roller rink, and the murder weapon was a trident. His primary suspect was Unity, the tour guide who Clarence had recently discovered was faking her knowledge about maritime history and planning to expose her. \n\nGathering himself, he strolled over to the corner, introducing himself to Unity, who was currently explaining to a group of tourists about the different types of tridents on display, her gaze fixated on the cases or wandering the distance, never meeting the eyes of her audience. She had the keys to the exhibits and thus unrestricted access to the tridents, a detail unbeknownst to the onlookers but not to Winston. \n\n\"I understand you\u2019re in charge of this trident exhibit.\u201d Winston pointed to the display case behind her. Unity glanced at the detective, showing a twinkling keychain, nodding. \"Yes, sir. I am the keeper of the keys around here,\" she confirmed, outwardly displaying a pride in her role.\n\nWinston started to probe: \"How did you end up in this line of work? You seem young...\" Unity took a moment, her gaze dropping to the ground. \"You could say it was my destiny,\" She replied, \"I've always been fascinated with the here, pretending to grasp every bit of maritime history...or at least pretending to do so\u2026\u201d\n\nPrintfessed, Winston furthered his inquiry. \u201cSo, you have exclusive access to these tridents, right?\u201d He meditated her reactions. Unity took a moment, her gaze deflecting to the ground and then at the tourists\u2019 faces. Faking her blithe demeanor, she corroborated, \u201cIndeed, I do. These tridents we have here are from various eras\u2014each belonging to a significant point in history and I am trained to handle them with utmost care. As a tour guide, I am responsible for explaining the heritage and significance of these artifacts.. \"\n\nUnity abruptly steered the conversation towards the artefacts and their historical context, evading Winston's direct questions. To an untrained eye, this would've made her look like an industrious professional. But Winston noticed her tactical evasion, making him ponder over how Clarence had noticed the same. He remembered how Clarence had confessed about Unity's deception before his untimely demise, her feigned knowledge about maritime history, her meticulously concealed secret, her meticulously fabricated response. \n\nWinston decided to step back for a bit, reassess his facts and clues. He had a gut feeling that both Clarence's death and Unity's secret were intertwined that this tour wasn't as benign as it seemed. He knew he was onto something. Unity's pride, her faked knowledge, her training with tridents, her constant evasion... While they weren't direct evidence, they were leading him somewhere.\n\nWith hands in his coat pockets, Winston retreated, mind abuzz with the next steps he needed to take in this intriguing roller rink homicide case. All the while under the riotous strobe lights, Unity's voice continued to echo through the space, meticulously detailing the care for tridents, her twisted pride in her position unfaltering. Even as they stood over a murder scene, she maintained the charade, pretending as if nothing had ever happened.\n\nDetective Winston scanned over the vivid crime scene for a moment, the macabre tableau horribly out of place in the otherwise cheery atmosphere of a roller rink. Luminescent skates scattered haphazardly over the checkered floors, their normally playful glow eerily reflec